<section class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">BEHIND THE INTELLIGENCE</span><h1>Beyond EPA. <em>Into robot intelligence.</em></h1><p>Neptune connects public match-derived ratings with detailed scouting observations to support better robot comparisons and match forecasts. Here's what each measure actually tells you—and what it doesn't.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">01 · THE FOUNDATION</span><h2>Public EPA — expected points added</h2><p>EPA estimates a team's contribution to alliance scoring from public match results. Neptune's public archive reports overall EPA alongside Autonomous, Teleoperated, and Endgame components. It's a historical performance estimate, not a guaranteed score for the next match.</p><p><a href="/epa/rankings.php">Explore public EPA rankings →</a></p></section>
<section class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">02 · DEFENSIVE IMPACT</span><h2>D-EPA — measuring defensive suppression</h2><p>Offense and defense aren't the same thing. Neptune tracks defensive impact separately, estimating how much scoring an opponent may have been prevented from achieving. SV-Points Denied adds context from scouting-confirmed defensive matches, evidence, and reliability measures. Defensive estimates can be noisy: one low-scoring opponent match does not prove one robot caused the difference.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">03 · LIVE EVENT FORM</span><h2>Scouting-Augmented EPA (SA-EPA)</h2><p>Public EPA provides an early baseline. As current-event scouting becomes available, Neptune blends observed scoring with that baseline. The model considers the event average, recent matches, upper-end performance, and measured trends. Scouting observations gain influence as the sample grows, helping a single unusually strong or weak match avoid dominating the rating.</p><div class="intel-compare"><div><b>Public EPA</b><span>Public match-derived baseline</span><p>Relatively independent of Neptune's private scouting observations.</p></div><div><b>SA-EPA</b><span>EPA + current-event scouting</span><p>Can rise or fall as scouts observe real scoring, recent form, and trends.</p></div></div><p class="intel-note">Illustration of two different estimates—not invented ratings for a real team. Actual numbers depend on available event data and the model's settings.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">04 · MATCH INTELLIGENCE</span><h2>AUGUR prediction modeling</h2><p>AUGUR brings team estimates together with available scouting observations, previous performance, and matchup context to estimate alliance performance and win probabilities. It also evaluates volatility, scoring trends, and observed defensive suppression. That observed defensive input should not be confused with automatically importing the separate, publicly displayed D-EPA value directly into every prediction.</p><p>Predictions are model-based estimates. Different alliance combinations, missing observations, robot problems, and unusual match events can all change actual outcomes.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">05 · CONFIDENCE & LIMITATIONS</span><h2>What confidence means</h2><p>More independent, consistent scouting observations can help Neptune move beyond an early baseline and better characterize a team's current event performance. More observations do not guarantee accuracy: data quality, scorer disagreement, schedule strength, and changing robot capabilities still matter.</p><p>Model confidence and win probability are different concepts. A 70% forecast does not mean a team will win 70% of its points or that the match is certain. Ratings and forecasts should be evaluated against actual results over time.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">06 · WHY NEPTUNE IS DIFFERENT</span><h2>From scouting evidence to strategy</h2><p>Neptune is more than an EPA leaderboard. Its authenticated scouting platform links robot-level observations, match phases, defense, pit notes, event operations, offline collection, and organization-specific strategy. These data inform the internal intelligence tools available to each organization while its private scouting records remain outside this public statistics website.</p><div class="tiles"><a class="tile" href="/epa/rankings.php"><b>Explore EPA</b><p>Public season rankings</p><span>Open →</span></a><a class="tile" href="/epa/team.php"><b>Find a team</b><p>Public performance history</p><span>Open →</span></a><a class="tile" href="/"><b>Explore Neptune</b><p>The full scouting platform</p><span>Open →</span></a></div></section>
<section class="panel"><span class="eyebrow">07 · FOR DEVELOPERS</span><h2>Use Neptune ratings in your app</h2><p>Neptune provides read-only public EPA and D-EPA JSON endpoints for teams, other scouting platforms, dashboards, and researchers. No scraping required. Public API responses do not include organization-private scouting records or Neptune's organization-scoped predicted EPA.</p><p><a href="/epa/api.php">View API endpoints and integration examples →</a></p></section>
